public final class Solution {
    public static Object solve(Map<String, Object> data) {
        List<String> board = (List<String>) data.get("board");
        for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
            Set<Character> seen = new HashSet<>();
            for (int j = 0; j < 9; j++) {
                char c = board.get(i).charAt(j);
                if (c != '.') {
                    if (seen.contains(c)) {
                        return false;
                    }
                    seen.add(c);
                }
            }
        }
        for (int j = 0; j < 9; j++) {
            Set<Character> seen = new HashSet<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
                char c = board.get(i).charAt(j);
                if (c != '.') {
                    if (seen.contains(c)) {
                        return false;
                    }
                    seen.add(c);
                }
            }
        }
        for (int boxRow = 0; boxRow < 9; boxRow += 3) {
            for (int boxCol = 0; boxCol < 9; boxCol += 3) {
                Set<Character> seen = new HashSet<>();
                for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {
                    for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {
                        char cell = board.get(boxRow + r).charAt(boxCol + c);
                        if (cell != '.') {
                            if (seen.contains(cell)) {
                                return false;
                            }
                            seen.add(cell);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Explanation
1. **Row Check**: The code iterates over each row. For each character in the row, if it's not a dot, it checks if the character has been seen before in that row. If duplicates are found, it immediately returns `false`.
2. **Column Check**: Similarly, the code checks each column by iterating over each row and the current column index. Duplicates in columns are detected and cause the function to return `false`.
3. **Box Check**: The board is divided into nine 3x3 boxes. For each box, the code checks all nine cells. If any digit is duplicated within a box, the function returns `false`.

